<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">St Andrews</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saint Andrews, Cennrigmonaid, Kinrimund</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">St Andrews is a town on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fife">Fife</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, known earlier as Cennrigmonaid. A Pictish church there received relics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-the-apostle">Saint Andrew</a> by tradition in the 8th century, and it became the seat of Scotland's chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, raised to an archbishopric in 1472. Its great cathedral now lies in ruins. The University of St Andrews, founded in 1413, is Scotland's oldest, and the town is known as the home of golf.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-andrews.jpg" alt="St Andrews" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Gordon · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Andrews_from_Regulus_tower_-_geograph.org.uk_-_254003.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>St Andrews lies on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fife">Fife</a>, 16 kilometres south-east of Dundee and 50 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>. It is named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-the-apostle">Saint Andrew</a> the Apostle, and the settlement grew to the west of the cathedral, between the Scores to the north and the Kinness Burn to the south. Its earliest recorded name occurs in 747, when an Irish annal noted the death of Tuathalán, abbot of Kinrymonth. The name combines the Old Gaelic cenn, head or peninsula, with Rigmonad, containing the words for king and moor, later rendered in Lowland Scots as King's Muir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first settlers on the estuaries of the Tay and the Eden arrived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a>, and farmers settled around the modern town about 4,500 BCE. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> a fortified enclosure, possibly a hillfort, stood on East Balrymonth hill a mile to the south, and it is the oldest surviving structure in the area. The burgh became the ecclesiastical capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, and held that position until the Scottish Reformation. Its cathedral, the largest in Scotland, is now a ruin. The Martyrs Memorial, at the west end of the Scores, honours Patrick Hamilton, George Wishart and other martyrs of the Reformation.</p><p>The town was once bounded by gates called ports, of which West Port, rebuilt between 1843 and 1845, and the Sea Yett survive. The Lade Braes Walk follows a medieval mill lade that may have been built before 1144. Fishing declined before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and the St Andrews Railway link to Leuchars ended in 1969.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>St Andrews had a population of 16,460 in 2022, and its economy rests heavily on tourism and education. It is home to the University of St Andrews, the oldest university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, and is known worldwide as the home of golf. The earliest surviving document, from 1552, records permission from Archbishop Hamilton to play golf on the links beside the water of Eden.</p><p>The Royal and Ancient Golf Club of St Andrews was founded in 1754 and until 2004 held legislative authority over the game outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. The town bought the Old Course in 1894, and it is the most frequent venue of the Open Championship, staged from 1873. The film <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">Chariots</a> of Fire used West Sands beach for its opening scene.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “St Andrews” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The church was rich, with great reformed abbeys such as Melrose, Kelso and Holyrood, and the founding of universities at <b>St Andrews</b> (1413), Glasgow (1451) and Aberdeen (1495) gave Scotland three universities when England still had two.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Pictish Christianity linked Iona and Northumbria, and the cult of St Andrew at <b>Kinrimund</b>, later St Andrews, which became Scotland’s chief bishopric and gave the country its patron saint, traditionally began under a Pictish king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The peninsula was fertile, and <b>St Andrews</b>, known earlier as Cennrigmonaid, became one of the leading Pictish churches, receiving relics of Saint Andrew by tradition in the 8th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/fib">Fib</a></li></ul></section>
